	"pages": [
		{
			"$type": "Page",
			"id": 1,
			"title": "Home",
			"hero": {
				"$type": "Hero",
				"type": "HIGH_IMPACT",
				"richText": "<h1>The vanilla foundation to build the lightweight web</h1><p>Janilla leverages standard technologies and principles to let you develop overengineering-free equivalent of web applications relying on heavy frameworks.</p>",
				"links": [
					{
						"$type": "LinkImpl",
						"type": "CUSTOM",
						"uri": "https://github.com/diego-schivo/janilla",
						"text": "Source Code",
						"appearance": "DEFAULT"
					}
				],
				"media": 4
			},
			"layout": [
				{
					"$type": "Content",
					"columns": [
						{
							"$type": "Column",
							"size": {
								"$type": "ColumnSize",
								"name": "FULL"
							},
							"richText": "<div>Janilla is a set of three libraries that implement an overengineering-free stack of web technologies.</div><div>- Base: provides IoC factory, JSON parser and formatter, web server and client, MVC factory.</div><div>- Backend: provides database, data persistence, CMS API, email sending.</div><div>- Frontend: provides templating engine, CMS administration panel.</div>"
						},
						{
							"$type": "Column",
							"size": {
								"$type": "ColumnSize",
								"name": "FULL"
							},
							"richText": "<div>Janilla is a self-contained solution.</div><div>- The base module has no dependencies (implicitly requires only the Java base module).</div><div>- The backend and frontend modules are independent of each other and require only the Janilla base module.</div>"
						},
						{
							"$type": "Column",
							"size": {
								"$type": "ColumnSize",
								"name": "FULL"
							},
							"richText": "<div>Janilla does not use any external systems other than the operating system.</div><div>The database and server are also embedded.</div>"
						},
						{
							"$type": "Column",
							"size": {
								"$type": "ColumnSize",
								"name": "FULL"
							},
							"richText": "<div>Janilla is not necessary a framework.</div><div>All library classes can work without IoC.</div>"
						},
						{
							"$type": "Column",
							"size": {
								"$type": "ColumnSize",
								"name": "FULL"
							},
							"richText": "<div>Janilla is a project written in the vanilla style of standard languages.</div><div>- Server-side: Java (Java SE 25 LTS).</div><div>- Client-side: JavaScript (ES6), HTML (HTML5), CSS (CSS3).</div>"
						},
						{
							"$type": "Column",
							"size": {
								"$type": "ColumnSize",
								"name": "FULL"
							},
							"richText": "<div>Janilla is a compact solution.</div><div>- 3 modules.</div><div>- 12 packages.</div><div>- 90 Java classes, 33 JavaScript classes.</div>"
						},
						{
							"$type": "Column",
							"size": {
								"$type": "ColumnSize",
								"name": "FULL"
							},
							"richText": "<div>Janilla provides everything you need to develop overengineering-free web applications.</div><div>- IoC factory: takes care of correctly building complex objects by retrieving the appropriate dependencies (DI).</div><div>- JSON parser and formatter: builds Java objects from JSON input and vice versa.</div><div>- Web server: accepts secure connections (TLS) from browser and converts between HTTP/2 streams and request/response objects.</div><div>- MVC factory: dispatches requests to handlers that implement the business logic and return responses, typically JSON (backend) or HTML/JS/CSS (frontend).</div><div>- Database: performs read and write operations on tables and indexes saved in a SQLite file.</div><div>- Data persistence: builds the database based on the entities that define the data model and takes care of saving and retrieving objects (CRUD).</div><div>- CMS API: extends data persistence by defining documents as entities with additional features (metadata/versions/drafts) and exposes access to them via REST API.</div><div>- Email sending: provides an SMTP client.</div><div>- Templating engine: implements JavaScript object rendering without direct DOM manipulation, using standard Web Components technologies.</div><div>- CMS administration panel: allows editors to manage application content through a web interface dynamically built from the data model.</div>"
						},
						{
							"$type": "Column",
							"size": {
								"$type": "ColumnSize",
								"name": "FULL"
							},
							"richText": "<div>Janilla is continuously improved by rewriting increasingly complex open-source applications originally developed by others.</div><div>- TodoMVC: with the templating engine, the UI can be easily created by leveraging only the power of JavaScript and HTML rather than resorting to more cumbersome alternative techniques.</div><div>- PetClinic: data persistence allows you to ignore the database and focus on the business model and logic, while SSR is achieved by combining POJOs, Java annotations and standard HTML.</div><div>- Address Book: a simple CRUD application that can run either as two separate processes (a frontend that calls the APIs exposed by the backend) or as a single fullstack process (convenient for development).</div><div>- Acme Dashboard: features JWT authentication and implements additional CRUD features such as search and pagination.</div><div>- RealWorld Conduit: features social features such as registration, comments, favorites and followers.</div><div>- Blank Template: the application integrates with the CMS and its admin panel, with users and media modeled as document collections.</div><div>- Website Template: the CMS maintains editorial content (pages, posts, images, categories) and non-editorial content (contact forms and requests, redirect rules, search results).</div><div>- Ecommerce Template: the CMS also maintains the catalog (products and variants) and additional user data related to their profile (addresses) and purchases (carts, orders, transactions).</div>"
						},
						{
							"$type": "Column",
							"size": {
								"$type": "ColumnSize",
								"name": "FULL"
							},
							"richText": "<div>Janilla is free software.</div><div>- The library license is the same as the OpenJDK implementation of Java SE.</div><div>- The reimplemented applications retained the same license as the corresponding original ones.</div>"
						}
					]
				}
			],
